## Orchestration (what to actually run)

{{ORCHESTRATION_DIAGRAM}}

**Write-back is the orchestrator's job for any subagent type without `Write`.** Check each
spawned `subagent_type`'s toolset before assuming it can produce its own output file — many
read-only agent types (search/explore/review agents) cannot write. For those, the
orchestrating session takes the `Agent` call's returned text and writes it verbatim to the
named path itself, before the next stage reads it.

{{OUTPUT_PATHS}}

Each stage's prompt must:
1. State its one question.
2. Name its output file path.
3. Require citations — no uncited claims.
{{ESCALATION_PROMPT_REQUIREMENT}}

**Gating is honest, not independent-checker gating.** Unless a stage explicitly says
otherwise, "whoever reads the output file" is the same orchestrating session that spawned
the stage — there is no separate checker agent for ordinary stages. That's an accepted
tradeoff for most workflows; only R1 below is a genuinely independent second pass.

## State

- [`TASKS.md`](TASKS.md) — the feature-list triples. Update `state` only when the named
  verification actually passes — the agent doing the work does not get to mark its own row
  `passing`; whoever reads its output file does.
- [`PROGRESS.md`](PROGRESS.md) — append one line per stage completed, with what was decided
  and why. This is what survives a context reset; reasoning does not survive unless it's
  written here.

## Review loop for this workflow design itself

This file set is treated as final only once R1 in `TASKS.md` is `passing` — see
`PROGRESS.md` for the review log. R1 is a manual step, not part of the pipeline above: spawn
one independent agent (any model — this is a design review, not part of the scored
pipeline) with no prior context on this draft, and have it review against
workflow-builder's `references/review-checklist.md`. Re-run it whenever this
file is materially edited later (new stage added, routing table changed) — it does not
re-trigger automatically.
